From: Kernel audit <littleddfu@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: hci_event: bound the key size from Read Encryption Key Size

hci_cc_read_enc_key_size() takes the encryption key size verbatim from a
Command Complete event and stores it into the persistent key material:

	conn->enc_key_size = rp->key_size;
	...
	if (conn->enc_key_size < hdev->min_enc_key_size ||
	    (key_enc_size && conn->enc_key_size < *key_enc_size)) { ... }
	...
	if (key_enc_size && *key_enc_size != conn->enc_key_size)
		*key_enc_size = conn->enc_key_size;

rp->key_size is a u8 taken off the wire and both checks are downgrade
checks, so by construction they permit arbitrarily large values.  For an
LE link hci_conn_key_enc_size() returns &ltk->enc_size of the stored
struct smp_ltk, whose val[] is 16 bytes.

b8dbe9648d69 ("Bluetooth: MGMT: validate LTK enc_size on load") closed
the MGMT load path to this sink by rejecting enc_size > sizeof(key->val)
in ltk_is_valid().  That check runs at install time only, so the write
above still reaches the same field afterwards and the same 16 byte sink
stays open.

hci_cmd_complete_evt() selects the handler purely from the opcode carried
in the event, with no correlation against a command the host actually
sent, so the Command Complete can be injected unsolicited by a local
process holding /dev/vhci.  hci_read_enc_key_size() is only ever sent for
ACL links, but the handler re-resolves the connection from an
attacker-chosen handle and does not re-check conn->type, so an LE
connection can be targeted.

hci_le_ltk_request_evt() then consumes the corrupted field as a length:

	struct hci_cp_le_ltk_reply cp;		/* 18 bytes, on the stack */
	memcpy(cp.ltk, ltk->val, ltk->enc_size);
	memset(cp.ltk + ltk->enc_size, 0, sizeof(cp.ltk) - ltk->enc_size);

giving, with enc_size = 0xff, a 255 byte read out of a 16 byte field, a
255 byte write into a 16 byte stack array, and a memset() whose length
16 - 255 underflows size_t:

  BUG: KASAN: slab-out-of-bounds in hci_le_ltk_request_evt+0x309/0xa00
  Read of size 255 at addr ffff88800d06f558 by task kworker/u5:0/65
   __asan_memcpy+0x29/0x70
   hci_le_ltk_request_evt+0x309/0xa00
   hci_event_packet+0x627/0xa80
   hci_rx_work+0x31c/0x700
  The buggy address belongs to the object at ffff88800d06f520
   which belongs to the cache kmalloc-96 of size 96
  The buggy address is located 56 bytes inside of
   allocated 72-byte region
  Allocated by task:
   hci_add_ltk+0x175/0x310
   load_long_term_keys+0x409/0x950

  UBSAN: array-index-out-of-bounds in net/bluetooth/hci_event.c:6721:16
  index 255 is out of range for type '__u8[16]'

Reject an out of range key size before it is stored, and add
HCI_MAX_ENC_KEY_SIZE alongside the existing HCI_MIN_ENC_KEY_SIZE.  Note
hci_conn_key_enc_size() also returns &link_key->pin_len for ACL links,
whose val[] is likewise 16 bytes, so the same bound covers both arms.

Fixes: c82b6357a546 ("Bluetooth: hci_event: Fix not using key encryption size when its known")

diff --git a/include/net/bluetooth/hci_core.h b/include/net/bluetooth/hci_core.h
index 3df59849dcbe..11e3d00e0f57 100644
--- a/include/net/bluetooth/hci_core.h
+++ b/include/net/bluetooth/hci_core.h
@@ -337,6 +337,11 @@ struct adv_monitor {
 
 /* Min encryption key size to match with SMP */
 #define HCI_MIN_ENC_KEY_SIZE		7
+/* Upper bound for a link encryption key size.  Both struct link_key and
+ * struct smp_ltk carry a 16 byte val[], and the stored key size is used as
+ * a memcpy() length against it.
+ */
+#define HCI_MAX_ENC_KEY_SIZE		16
 
 /* Default LE RPA expiry time, 15 minutes */
 #define HCI_DEFAULT_RPA_TIMEOUT		(15 * 60)
diff --git a/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c b/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
index 741d658e9630..764a6e7e8fcb 100644
--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
@@ -742,6 +742,20 @@ static u8 hci_cc_read_enc_key_size(struct hci_dev *hdev, void *data,
 	} else {
 		u8 *key_enc_size = hci_conn_key_enc_size(conn);
 
+		/* The key size comes straight off the wire and is stored into
+		 * the persistent key material below.  ltk_is_valid() bounds it
+		 * when a key is loaded over MGMT, but that runs at install
+		 * time only, so an out of range value arriving here would be
+		 * written past it and later used as a memcpy() length against
+		 * a 16 byte buffer.  Reject it before it is stored.
+		 */
+		if (rp->key_size > HCI_MAX_ENC_KEY_SIZE) {
+			bt_dev_err(hdev, "invalid key size %u for handle %u",
+				   rp->key_size, handle);
+			status = HCI_ERROR_AUTH_FAILURE;
+			goto done;
+		}
+
 		conn->enc_key_size = rp->key_size;
 		status = 0;
 
